== 17:10 Item 4 is built, correct in shape, and EMPTY in civ2civ3 ==

Probe, 40-turn contested, bundle ini, all six gains at 1.0:

    deep-upcharge   309      deep-deter      18   (marg 11.3 shields,
    deep-popins      11      deep-deter-sum  18    menace 1.000)
    deep-sortie       7      deep-sortie-no   1
    deep-barracks     0   <-- nothing

Chased it down rather than blaming the 40-turn probe, and the answer
is not about turns.

1. civ2civ3's Barracks HAS both effects. It is buildable from turn 1
   (no reqs at all, buildings.ruleset:262). So the credit functions
   are reached -- 363 times for Barracks in one game.

2. But the effects are gated on a BUILDING FLAG, not a building:

       [effect_barracks_veteran]  type = "Veteran_Build"  value = 1
         reqs = { "BuildingFlag", "Barracks", "City", TRUE
                  "UnitClassFlag", "Barracks", "Local", TRUE ... }
       [effect_barracks_regen]    type = "HP_Regen"       value = 100
         reqs = { "BuildingFlag", "Barracks", "City", TRUE ... }

   (effects.ruleset:1824 and :1835. Three buildings carry the flag:
   Barracks, Barracks II, Barracks III.)

3. `get_potential_improvement_bonus()` finds effects through
   `get_req_source_effects()` keyed on a VUT_IMPROVEMENT source. An
   effect whose requirement is VUT_IMPR_FLAG is indexed under a
   different source and is NEVER RETURNED. Measured directly:

       DBGV impr="Barracks" vb_poss=0 vb_cert=0 hp_poss=0
            have_vb=0 have_hp=0        (363 times, one game)

   RPT_POSSIBLE instead of RPT_CERTAIN does not help; I tried it and
   it is still 0. The problem is the index, not the problem type.

**THE FINDING THAT IS BIGGER THAN ITEM 4:
`get_potential_improvement_bonus()` is blind to every effect a
ruleset gates on a building FLAG rather than a building.** Deep uses
that accessor in the growth credit, the health credit, the content
credit and now these two. Any flag-gated effect in any ruleset is
priced at zero by all of them. This is very likely why stock prices
HP_Regen and Veteran_Build with headcount WAGs in daieffects.c
instead of reading them -- and daieffects.c:479 carries the matching
FIXME, "check other reqs (e.g., unitflag)".

NOT FIXED HERE, deliberately. Doing it right needs the effect
evaluated against a context that knows the candidate building's
flags -- a virtual city, or the `struct req_context` field 00-context
section 3 already lists as future work. That is a slice, not a
20-minute patch at the end of a session, and rushing shared
requirement-evaluation code is how this project breaks its control.

Item 4 therefore ships built, off, and honestly reported as
zero-population in civ2civ3. Its arithmetic is unexercised, so its
two knobs are untuned AND untested against live data.

I reverted the experiment so the binary went back to matching the
pinned one (both cff0da39e9dd3d), which kept the 80-game run valid
rather than costing a third restart.

